ARTICLES = [
dict(slug='moon-garden', cat='questions', img='moon', support=False,
 title="""Could you grow a garden on the Moon?""",
 excerpt="""Light, soil, water and air: what plants need, what the Moon is missing, and what scientists have already tried.""",
 desc="""What would it take to grow plants on the Moon? Light, soil, water and air explained, plus the real experiments scientists have already done.""",
 lead="""Maybe one day, with a lot of help. Here is what plants need to grow, what the Moon is missing, and what scientists have already tried.""",
 body='''<p>Plants need four big things: light, water, air and something to grow in. On Earth we get all four almost for free. On the Moon, every single one is a puzzle.</p>
<h2>1. Light</h2><p>The Moon gets plenty of sunshine, but its days are very long. In most places, about two Earth weeks of daylight are followed by about two weeks of darkness. A Moon garden would need lamps to get through those long, freezing nights.</p>
<h2>2. Air and water</h2><p>The Moon has almost no atmosphere, so there is no air to breathe and nothing to keep warmth in. Any garden would have to live inside a sealed, heated greenhouse. Water is precious too. Scientists think there is ice hiding in dark craters near the Moon's poles, which future explorers might be able to use.</p>
<h2>3. Soil</h2><p>Moon "soil" is called regolith. It is a dusty, sharp powder made of crushed rock, with none of the worms, fungi and rotting leaves that make Earth soil so rich. In 2022, scientists at the University of Florida tried growing a small plant called thale cress in tiny samples of real regolith brought back by the Apollo astronauts. The seeds sprouted, which was exciting, but the plants grew slowly and showed signs of stress compared with plants grown in Earth-like soil.</p>
<blockquote>Big question: what would YOU plant first?</blockquote>
<h2>4. Has anything grown on the Moon?</h2><p>Yes, briefly. In 2019, China's Chang'e 4 lander carried a small sealed container to the far side of the Moon with seeds inside. A cotton seed sprouted, the first plant ever to sprout on the Moon, but it did not survive the cold lunar night.</p>
<h2>Try it at home</h2><ol><li><strong>Plant:</strong> sow cress seeds in two pots, one with sand and one with potting mix.</li><li><strong>Record:</strong> measure how tall they grow every day for a week.</li><li><strong>Think:</strong> which pot did better, and why? That is the same question space scientists are asking.</li></ol>'''),

dict(slug='t-shirt-tote-bag', cat='make', img='tote-bag', support=False,
 title="""Turn an old T-shirt into a tote bag in 20 minutes""",
 excerpt="""No sewing machine needed: a pair of scissors, a few knots and you have a bag with a story.""",
 desc="""Make a no-sew tote bag from an old T-shirt with just scissors and knots. A simple 20-minute upcycling project.""",
 lead="""That T-shirt you love but never wear? It can have a second life as a bag. No sewing machine, no needle, just scissors and a few knots.""",
 body='''<h2>You will need</h2><ul><li>An old T-shirt (cotton works best; ask before you cut anything that isn't yours)</li><li>Sharp fabric scissors</li><li>A ruler and a pen, chalk or marker</li><li>A bowl or plate to trace around</li></ul>
<h2>Steps</h2><ol>
<li><strong>Turn it inside out</strong> and lay it flat. Your knots will end up hidden on the inside.</li>
<li><strong>Cut off the sleeves</strong>, just inside the seams. The holes become the tops of your handles.</li>
<li><strong>Cut the neckline.</strong> Place a bowl over the neck, trace around it and cut. A bigger scoop makes a wider opening for the bag.</li>
<li><strong>Decide the depth.</strong> Draw a line across the bottom of the shirt where you want the base of the bag. Above this line it stays a bag; below it becomes fringe.</li>
<li><strong>Cut the fringe.</strong> Cut strips about 2 cm wide from the bottom hem up to your line, through both layers.</li>
<li><strong>Tie it closed.</strong> Tie each front strip to the back strip directly behind it with a double knot. Then tie each pair to the pair next door so there are no gaps.</li>
<li><strong>Turn it right side out</strong>, and that's your tote.</li></ol>
<blockquote>Every bag you make is one less shirt in the bin.</blockquote>
<h2>Make it yours</h2><p>Decorate it with fabric paint or fabric markers, iron-on patches, or a stencil cut from cardboard. Or leave the original print on show: a faded band tee or a shirt from a school camp makes a tote with a story.</p>
<p>A T-shirt bag is stretchy, so it is perfect for library books, swimming gear or a picnic, but don't overload it with anything too heavy.</p>'''),

dict(slug='why-is-the-sky-blue', cat='questions', img='sunset-sky', support=False,
 title="""Why is the sky blue (and sunsets orange)?""",
 excerpt="""A tiny bit of physics explains one of the prettiest things you see every day.""",
 desc="""Why is the sky blue and why are sunsets orange and pink? A simple explanation of how sunlight scatters in the air.""",
 lead="""It is one of the oldest questions people ask, and the answer is hiding in the air all around you.""",
 body='''<h2>Sunlight is secretly every colour</h2><p>Sunlight looks white, but it is really a mix of all the colours of the rainbow. You can see this when light shines through a prism or raindrops and spreads out into a rainbow. Each colour is light travelling as a wave of a different length: red has the longest waves, and blue and violet have the shortest.</p>
<h2>The air bounces blue around</h2><p>Air is made of tiny molecules, mostly nitrogen and oxygen. When sunlight hits them, some of it gets scattered, bounced off in every direction. Short, blue waves get scattered much more than long, red waves. So when you look at any part of the sky away from the Sun, you are seeing blue light that has been bounced across the sky towards your eyes. Scientists call this <strong>Rayleigh scattering</strong>, after the British physicist Lord Rayleigh, who worked out the maths in the 1800s.</p>
<h2>Then why isn't the sky violet?</h2><p>Violet scatters even more than blue, so this is a great question. There are a few reasons: the Sun sends out less violet light than blue, some violet is absorbed high in the atmosphere, and our eyes are much more sensitive to blue. Put together, our brains see a blue sky.</p>
<blockquote>Every sunset is physics putting on a show.</blockquote>
<h2>Why sunsets glow orange and pink</h2><p>When the Sun is low, its light has to travel through much more air to reach you. By the time it arrives, most of the blue has been scattered away, leaving the oranges and reds. Dust, smoke and tiny water droplets can make sunsets even more colourful.</p>
<h2>Try it</h2><p>Fill a clear glass with water and stir in a few drops of milk. Shine a torch through the side in a dark room. From the side, the water looks slightly blue; looking through it towards the torch, the light looks orange. You've made a tiny sky.</p>
<p><strong>Safety:</strong> never look straight at the Sun, even at sunset.</p>'''),

dict(slug='five-minute-wind-down', cat='mind', img='tea', support=True,
 title="""A five-minute wind-down for busy brains""",
 excerpt="""Three simple steps to help you switch off before bed, without needing an app.""",
 desc="""A simple five-minute wind-down routine to help teens switch off before bed: put screens away, empty your head and slow your breathing.""",
 lead="""Lying in bed with your brain still racing? You are definitely not the only one. Here is a short routine you can try tonight. No app needed.""",
 body='''<p>Health experts generally recommend that teenagers get around 8 to 10 hours of sleep a night, but a busy head can make switching off hard. This routine takes about five minutes. Try it for a week and see how it feels.</p>
<h2>Step 1: Dim the lights (1 minute)</h2><p>Put your phone on the other side of the room, or at least face down with notifications off. Turn off bright lights and switch on a lamp. Bright screens and lights can make your brain think it is still daytime. A warm, caffeine-free drink like warm milk or a herbal tea can be a cosy signal that the day is done.</p>
<h2>Step 2: Brain dump (2 minutes)</h2><p>Grab a notebook and write down everything rattling around in your head: tomorrow's to-dos, something that annoyed you, an idea you don't want to forget. It doesn't have to be neat. Once it is on paper, your brain doesn't have to keep holding onto it.</p>
<blockquote>Thoughts on paper are thoughts you don't have to hold onto.</blockquote>
<h2>Step 3: Slow breathing (2 minutes)</h2><p>Sit or lie comfortably. Breathe in gently through your nose while you count to four, then breathe out slowly while you count to six. Breathing out for longer than you breathe in helps many people feel calmer. If your mind wanders, that's normal: just go back to counting.</p>
<h2>Bonus: the body scan</h2><p>Still awake? Starting at your toes, squeeze each part of your body for a few seconds and then let it go floppy: feet, legs, tummy, hands, shoulders, face.</p>
<h2>When it's more than a busy brain</h2><p>Everyone has the odd bad night. But if you are often lying awake worrying, or feeling down or anxious most days, you deserve support. Talk to a parent, carer, teacher or school counsellor, or reach out to one of the free services below.</p>'''),

dict(slug='start-a-coding-club', cat='voice', img='code', support=False,
 title="""How to start a coding club at lunch""",
 excerpt="""A room, a teacher and a first project: a step-by-step guide to starting your own coding crew.""",
 desc="""A step-by-step guide to starting a lunchtime coding club at school, with free beginner tools and first project ideas.""",
 lead="""Wish your school had a coding club? You could be the one to start it. You don't need to be an expert, just curious and ready to learn alongside everyone else.""",
 body='''<h2>1. Find a teacher on your side</h2><p>Most schools need a staff member to supervise a lunchtime club. Ask a digital technologies, maths or science teacher, or the librarian. Explain what you want to do and when. A short, friendly pitch works well: "I'd like to start a coding club for beginners on Wednesdays. Could we use your room?"</p>
<h2>2. Pick a free tool</h2><ul>
<li><strong>Scratch</strong> (scratch.mit.edu): drag-and-drop blocks, great for games and animations. It is free and was created at the MIT Media Lab.</li>
<li><strong>Code.org</strong>: free step-by-step courses and short "Hour of Code" activities.</li>
<li><strong>Python</strong>: a real text-based language that's popular with beginners. Ask your teacher which editor the school computers can use.</li></ul>
<p>Check with your teacher about using school devices and accounts, and keep club work on school-approved sites.</p>
<h2>3. Plan a fun first session</h2><p>Keep it short and hands-on. Something everyone can finish in one lunch, like a sprite that moves when you press the arrow keys, or a quiz that keeps score, makes people want to come back.</p>
<blockquote>Nobody starts out knowing how to code. Everyone starts out curious.</blockquote>
<h2>4. Spread the word</h2><p>Ask if you can put a poster in the library or an announcement in the school newsletter. Make it clear beginners are welcome. Lots of people stay away from coding because they think everyone else already knows how.</p>
<h2>5. Keep it going</h2><ul><li>Have a goal, like building a game for a school fair.</li><li>Take turns choosing projects.</li><li>Pair up experienced coders with beginners.</li><li>Celebrate when things work, and when bugs get squashed.</li></ul>
<p>Started a club or project you are proud of? Read <a href="submit.html">how sharing with Your Voice will work</a>.</p>'''),

dict(slug='six-adventure-books', cat='rwl', img='reading', support=False,
 title="""Six books to read when you want an adventure""",
 excerpt="""From dragons to wild forests: six adventure stories worth getting lost in.""",
 desc="""Six adventure books for teens, from The Hobbit to Nevermoor: quests, magic, survival and space travel.""",
 lead="""Some days you want a book that grabs you by the hand and runs. Here are six adventure stories, old and new, to get lost in.""",
 body='''<h2>1. The Hobbit by J.R.R. Tolkien</h2><p>Bilbo Baggins loves his cosy hole and his second breakfasts, until a wizard and thirteen dwarves turn up and sweep him off on a quest to a mountain guarded by the dragon Smaug. First published in 1937 and still a brilliant place to start.</p>
<h2>2. Nevermoor: The Trials of Morrigan Crow by Jessica Townsend</h2><p>Morrigan Crow is cursed and doomed to die on her eleventh birthday, until she is whisked away to the magical city of Nevermoor and has to compete in a series of dangerous trials. Written by an Australian author and full of wit and wonder.</p>
<h2>3. A Wrinkle in Time by Madeleine L'Engle</h2><p>Meg Murry is stubborn, clever and convinced she doesn't fit in. When her scientist father disappears, she travels across space and time to find him. A classic from 1962 about courage and love.</p>
<blockquote>The best adventures make you braver in real life too.</blockquote>
<h2>4. Northern Lights by Philip Pullman</h2><p>Lyra Belacqua lives in a world where every person has an animal companion called a dæmon. When children start vanishing, Lyra heads to the frozen North to find out why. Published as <em>The Golden Compass</em> in some countries.</p>
<h2>5. The Girl Who Drank the Moon by Kelly Barnhill</h2><p>A kind witch accidentally feeds a baby moonlight, filling her with magic she doesn't understand. A gentle, magical story that won the Newbery Medal in 2017.</p>
<h2>6. Hatchet by Gary Paulsen</h2><p>After a small plane crashes in the Canadian wilderness, Brian is alone with nothing but a hatchet. A gripping survival story about patience, mistakes and working things out for yourself.</p>
<h2>Where to find them</h2><p>Your school or local library is the best place to start. If a book is out, librarians can often reserve it for you. Some libraries also lend eBooks and audiobooks through free apps.</p>'''),

dict(slug='talk-money-with-your-family', cat='world', img='money-jar', support=False,
 title="""How to talk money with your family""",
 excerpt="""Pocket money, first jobs and saving up: conversation starters that don't feel awkward.""",
 desc="""Easy conversation starters for teens to talk about pocket money, saving and first jobs with their family.""",
 lead="""Money can feel like a grown-ups-only topic, but learning about it early is one of the most useful things you can do. Here is how to start the conversation without it feeling weird.""",
 body='''<h2>Pick a good moment</h2><p>Not in the middle of a busy morning, and not straight after asking for something expensive. A car trip, a walk or helping cook dinner are all good times for a relaxed chat.</p>
<h2>Conversation starters</h2><ul>
<li>"What was the first thing you ever saved up for?"</li>
<li>"How did you learn about money when you were my age?"</li>
<li>"Could I earn some money by helping out with extra jobs?"</li>
<li>"Can you show me how you plan the shopping budget?"</li>
<li>"What's one money mistake you wish someone had warned you about?"</li></ul>
<p>Everyone's family situation is different, and some families don't like to talk about money. That's okay. Asking about how they learned, rather than what they earn, usually feels more comfortable.</p>
<blockquote>Asking questions about money is a grown-up move.</blockquote>
<h2>Saving up for something</h2><p>Choose a goal, work out the cost, and divide it into small weekly amounts. A clear jar, a chart on your wall or a simple spreadsheet lets you watch your progress grow. Some people split any money they get into three parts: spend, save and give.</p>
<h2>Thinking about a first job?</h2><p>Babysitting younger cousins, pet-sitting for neighbours you know, or helping out in a family business are common first steps. Rules about what age you can start paid work, and what kinds of jobs are allowed, depend on which Australian state or territory you live in. Check with a parent or carer, and look at the Fair Work Ombudsman website (fairwork.gov.au) for information about young workers.</p>
<h2>Learn more</h2><p>The Australian Government's free Moneysmart website (moneysmart.gov.au) has easy guides on budgeting, saving and banking. Explore it with an adult.</p>'''),

dict(slug='no-bake-oat-cookies', cat='make', img='cookies', support=False,
 title="""Easy no-bake cookies for a rainy afternoon""",
 excerpt="""Five ingredients, one bowl and zero oven time.""",
 desc="""A simple no-bake cookie recipe with five ingredients, one bowl and no oven: a fun rainy-day kitchen project.""",
 lead="""Rainy afternoon, nothing to do? These chewy chocolate oat cookies need five ingredients, one bowl and no oven at all.""",
 body='''<h2>You will need</h2><ul>
<li>1½ cups rolled oats</li>
<li>½ cup smooth peanut butter (or sunflower seed butter if anyone has a nut allergy)</li>
<li>⅓ cup honey</li>
<li>2 tablespoons cocoa powder</li>
<li>¼ cup chocolate chips</li>
<li>A big bowl, a spoon, a tray and baking paper</li></ul>
<p><strong>Allergy check:</strong> always ask before making food for other people, and check every label if someone has an allergy.</p>
<h2>Steps</h2><ol>
<li><strong>Wash your hands</strong> and line a tray with baking paper.</li>
<li><strong>Mix the wet stuff.</strong> Stir the peanut butter, honey and cocoa together in the bowl until smooth and glossy. If the peanut butter is very stiff, ask an adult to help you warm it for a few seconds in the microwave.</li>
<li><strong>Add the oats</strong> and stir until every oat is coated. It takes a bit of muscle!</li>
<li><strong>Fold in the chocolate chips.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Shape.</strong> Roll tablespoon-sized balls with your hands, put them on the tray and press each one flat with the back of a spoon.</li>
<li><strong>Chill</strong> in the fridge for about 30 minutes until firm.</li></ol>
<blockquote>Best eaten with friends, a blanket and the sound of rain.</blockquote>
<h2>Make it your own</h2><ul><li>Swap the chocolate chips for sultanas or shredded coconut.</li><li>Add a pinch of cinnamon.</li><li>Roll them in coconut or sprinkles before chilling.</li></ul>
<p>Keep them in a container in the fridge and eat them within a few days. Then the most important step: clean up the kitchen!</p>'''),

dict(slug='living-on-mars', cat='questions', img='mars', support=False,
 title="""What would it be like to live on Mars?""",
 excerpt="""Air, food, gravity and the very long commute, explained.""",
 desc="""What would daily life on Mars be like? The air, the cold, the gravity, the long trip and how scientists plan to solve them.""",
 lead="""Space agencies and companies are dreaming about people living on Mars one day. So what would a normal day on the red planet actually be like?""",
 body='''<h2>The commute</h2><p>Mars is far away, and the distance keeps changing as both planets orbit the Sun. With today's rockets, the trip takes roughly six to nine months. Launches are planned for when the planets line up well, which happens about every two years.</p>
<h2>The air</h2><p>You couldn't breathe outside. Mars's atmosphere is very thin, with less than one per cent of the air pressure we have on Earth, and it is mostly carbon dioxide. Settlers would need spacesuits outdoors and sealed homes inside. One clever experiment: NASA's Perseverance rover carried a device called MOXIE that successfully made small amounts of oxygen from Martian carbon dioxide.</p>
<h2>The weather</h2><p>Bring a jacket. A really, really good one. Mars is much colder than Earth, with an average temperature of around minus 60 °C. It also has huge dust storms that can sometimes cover the whole planet.</p>
<blockquote>On Mars, the sunsets are blue. (Look it up. It's real.)</blockquote>
<h2>Gravity and days</h2><p>Mars's gravity is about 38 per cent of Earth's, so you could jump much higher. A Martian day, called a sol, is about 24 hours and 40 minutes long, so you'd get a slightly longer sleep-in. But a Martian year lasts 687 Earth days, so birthdays would come around much less often.</p>
<h2>Food and phone calls</h2><p>Food would have to be brought from Earth or grown in greenhouses, which is why scientists study growing plants in tough conditions. And calling home wouldn't be instant: radio messages take between about 3 and 22 minutes to travel one way, depending on where the planets are.</p>
<h2>Protection</h2><p>Mars has no global magnetic field and a thin atmosphere, so more radiation from space reaches the surface. Homes might be built underground or covered with thick layers of Martian soil.</p>
<p>Would you go? Maybe the first person to walk on Mars is reading this right now.</p>'''),

dict(slug='desk-stretches', cat='mind', img='stretching', support=True,
 title="""Stretches you can do at your desk""",
 excerpt="""Loosen up your neck, shoulders and wrists between study sessions.""",
 desc="""Gentle stretches teens can do at a desk to loosen up neck, shoulders, wrists and back between study sessions.""",
 lead="""Homework, gaming, scrolling: we spend a lot of time sitting still. These gentle stretches take a couple of minutes and you don't even have to leave your chair.""",
 body='''<p>Move slowly, breathe normally and never stretch to the point of pain. A stretch should feel like a gentle pull, not a hurt. If anything is sore or you have an injury, check with a parent, carer or doctor first.</p>
<h2>1. Neck tilts</h2><p>Sit tall. Slowly tilt your right ear towards your right shoulder and hold for a few breaths. Come back to the middle and repeat on the left. Keep your shoulders relaxed and down.</p>
<h2>2. Shoulder rolls</h2><p>Lift your shoulders up towards your ears, roll them back and down, then forwards. Do five big slow circles each way.</p>
<h2>3. Wrist and finger stretch</h2><p>Hold one arm out in front with your palm facing away, like a stop sign. Use your other hand to gently draw your fingers back towards you. Hold, then swap. Finish by making fists and spreading your fingers wide a few times. Great after lots of typing or drawing.</p>
<blockquote>Your body was made to move, not to stay frozen in one position all day.</blockquote>
<h2>4. Seated twist</h2><p>Sit with both feet flat on the floor. Place your right hand on the outside of your left knee and gently turn your upper body to the left. Hold for a few breaths, come back to centre, and twist the other way.</p>
<h2>5. Ankle circles</h2><p>Lift one foot off the floor and draw slow circles with your toes, five each way. Swap feet.</p>
<h2>6. Reach for the sky</h2><p>Stand up if you can, link your fingers and stretch your arms up over your head. Take a big breath in and let it out slowly.</p>
<h2>Make it a habit</h2><p>Try stretching at the end of each study block, or whenever you finish a chapter or a level. Getting up for a glass of water or a quick walk around the house counts too.</p>'''),

dict(slug='group-chat-drama', cat='world', img='phone', support=True,
 title="""Group chat drama? Try these three steps""",
 excerpt="""How to step back, speak up or log off when chats get heated.""",
 desc="""What to do when a group chat gets heated: three steps for teens to step back, speak up kindly or log off, and where to get help.""",
 lead="""One message gets misread, someone gets left out, and suddenly the whole group chat is on fire. Here is what you can do.""",
 body='''<h2>Step 1: Step back</h2><p>Before you reply, pause. Put the phone down, take a breath and come back later. Messages are easy to misread because you can't hear someone's tone or see their face. Ask yourself: "Would I say this to their face? Will this still matter tomorrow?" Muting the chat for a few hours is completely allowed.</p>
<h2>Step 2: Speak up (kindly)</h2><p>If something is bothering you, it often helps to message the person privately or talk in person instead of arguing in front of everyone. Try "I" sentences: "I felt left out when…" works better than "You always…". If someone else is being picked on, a simple "Hey, that's not cool" or a private message to them saying "Are you okay?" can make a big difference.</p>
<blockquote>You are allowed to log off. Your peace matters more than the last word.</blockquote>
<h2>Step 3: Log off</h2><p>If a chat keeps making you feel bad, you can leave it, mute it or block people. A real friend won't make you stay somewhere that hurts. Spend time with the people who make you feel good, online and off.</p>
<h2>When it's bullying</h2><p>If someone is being mean again and again, sharing private photos, threatening anyone or ganging up on someone, that's not drama: it's bullying, and it's not your fault.</p><ul>
<li><strong>Don't reply</strong> or get into an argument.</li>
<li><strong>Save evidence:</strong> take screenshots, including dates and names.</li>
<li><strong>Tell a trusted adult</strong>, like a parent, carer, teacher or school counsellor.</li>
<li><strong>Report and block</strong> using the app's tools.</li>
<li>In Australia, the <strong>eSafety Commissioner</strong> (esafety.gov.au) can help with serious cyberbullying and getting harmful posts removed.</li></ul>
<p>And if a chat has left you feeling upset, anxious or alone, please talk to someone. You can contact one of the free services below at any time.</p>'''),

dict(slug='reader-art-wall-home', cat='voice', img='art-wall', support=False,
 title="""Reader art wall: theme &quot;Home&quot;""",
 excerpt="""A drawing, photo or collage about what home means to you. Here is the theme and some ideas to get you started.""",
 desc="""Rebella's first reader art wall theme is "Home". Ideas for drawings, collages and photos, plus how sharing with Rebella will work safely.""",
 lead="""Our first art wall theme is "Home". Home can be a place, a person, a smell, a song or a feeling. What does it mean to you?""",
 body='''<h2>Ideas to get you started</h2><ul>
<li>Draw your favourite corner, like your bed, a window seat or a tree you climb.</li>
<li>Make a collage of colours and textures that feel like home.</li>
<li>Photograph small details: a mug, your shoes by the door, the light through a curtain.</li>
<li>Paint a place you've lived, or a place you dream of living one day.</li>
<li>Design a map of everywhere that has ever felt like home.</li>
<li>Write a short poem and decorate the page.</li></ul>
<p>Need a starting point? Try our <a href="create.html">Create page</a> to make a mood board of photos and stickers, then save it as a picture.</p>
<blockquote>Home isn't always a place. Sometimes it's a feeling.</blockquote>
<h2>How sharing will work</h2><p>Rebella's art wall isn't open for sending in work just yet. We are setting it up carefully so it is safe for everyone. When it opens:</p><ul>
<li>A parent or carer will need to say it's okay before you send anything.</li>
<li>Every piece is checked and approved by an adult editor before anything goes on the site.</li>
<li>We only ever show a first name or a made-up artist name. Never your full name, school, suburb or photos of your face.</li>
<li>There are no comments or likes. The wall is just for enjoying each other's work.</li></ul>
<h2>Keep yourself safe in your art</h2><p>Before you share any picture, anywhere, check it doesn't show your address, school uniform or logo, street signs, your house number or anyone who hasn't said it's okay to be in it.</p>
<p>In the meantime, start making! Keep your piece somewhere safe, and keep an eye on Rebella for when the wall opens.</p>'''),

dict(slug='paint-a-door-mural', cat='make', img='mural', support=False,
 title="""Paint a mini mural on your bedroom door (with permission!)""",
 excerpt="""Plan, sketch, paint, admire.""",
 desc="""How to plan and paint a mini mural on your bedroom door, or a removable version on card, step by step and with permission.""",
 lead="""Want to turn a plain door into a work of art? A mini mural is a big, satisfying project. Just make sure you have a yes from whoever owns the door first!""",
 body='''<h2>Step 0: Ask first</h2><p>Paint is hard to undo, and if you rent, the landlord may need to agree too. Show your parent or carer your plan. If painting the door itself is a no, there is a great removable option: paint your mural on a large sheet of thick card or a thin board, then hang it on the door with removable hooks or tape.</p>
<h2>1. Plan your design</h2><p>Measure the space and draw a small version on paper first, to scale if you can. Simple, bold shapes like flowers, waves, stars, mountains or a big word look great from across the room. Choose a palette of three to five colours so it looks pulled together.</p>
<h2>2. Get the right supplies</h2><ul><li>Water-based acrylic paints (easy to clean up with water)</li><li>Brushes in a few sizes and a small sponge</li><li>Painter's tape for crisp edges</li><li>A drop sheet or old newspaper</li><li>A pencil or chalk for sketching</li></ul>
<h2>3. Prepare the surface</h2><p>With an adult's help, take the door handle off or cover it with tape. Wipe the door clean and let it dry. Open a window so the room is well ventilated.</p>
<blockquote>Every mural starts with a tiny sketch.</blockquote>
<h2>4. Sketch, then paint</h2><p>Lightly sketch the outline onto the door. Paint the big background shapes first, then the smaller details on top once they're dry. Thin layers dry faster and look smoother than one thick coat. Pull off tape while the paint is still slightly wet for sharp lines.</p>
<h2>5. Finish and admire</h2><p>Add outlines or tiny details with a fine brush or paint pen. Clean your brushes with water straight away. Then step back and admire it. You made that!</p>'''),

dict(slug='sketchbook-habit', cat='make', img='pencils', support=False,
 title="""Start a sketchbook habit you'll actually keep""",
 excerpt="""Ten-minute prompts for every day of the week.""",
 desc="""Build a sketchbook habit with ten-minute drawing prompts for every day of the week. No talent or fancy supplies needed.""",
 lead="""Drawing gets easier the more you do it. The secret isn't talent or fancy supplies, it's showing up for ten minutes. Here's a week of prompts to start.""",
 body='''<h2>The ground rules</h2><ul><li><strong>Any sketchbook works.</strong> A cheap notebook is perfect, because you won't worry about "wasting" pages.</li><li><strong>Ten minutes.</strong> Set a timer. Small and regular beats big and rare.</li><li><strong>No ripping out pages.</strong> Wobbly drawings are proof you are practising.</li><li><strong>Date each page,</strong> so later you can flip back and see how far you've come.</li></ul>
<h2>A week of prompts</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Monday: What's on your desk?</strong> Draw three objects within arm's reach, exactly as they are.</li>
<li><strong>Tuesday: Blind contour.</strong> Draw your hand without looking at the page. It will look silly, and that's the point. It trains your eye.</li>
<li><strong>Wednesday: Five-minute faces.</strong> Draw characters from a favourite show or book, or invent new ones.</li>
<li><strong>Thursday: Out the window.</strong> Sketch the view: a tree, a rooftop, the clouds.</li>
<li><strong>Friday: Colour only.</strong> No lines at all. Fill a page with shapes of colour that match your mood.</li>
<li><strong>Saturday: Mash-up.</strong> Combine two random things, like a cat and a teapot, or a whale and a bicycle.</li>
<li><strong>Sunday: Redo.</strong> Pick a drawing from earlier in the week and draw it again. Notice what improved.</li></ol>
<blockquote>Done is better than perfect. Every page counts.</blockquote>
<h2>Staying motivated</h2><p>Keep your sketchbook and a pencil somewhere you will see them, like on your pillow or next to your school bag. Pair drawing with something you already do, like after dinner or while listening to music. Missed a day? No problem. Just start again tomorrow.</p>
<p>Want more ideas? Make a mood board of colours and pictures you love on our <a href="create.html">Create page</a> and use it as inspiration.</p>'''),

dict(slug='three-ingredient-lip-balm', cat='make', img='honey', support=False,
 title="""Make your own lip balm with three ingredients""",
 excerpt="""A simple kitchen-chemistry project using beeswax and honey.""",
 desc="""Make a simple homemade lip balm with beeswax, coconut oil and honey: a kitchen chemistry project to do with an adult.""",
 lead="""Lip balm is a mini chemistry experiment you can actually use. This one needs just three ingredients, and an adult to help with the hot part.""",
 body='''<h2>You will need</h2><ul>
<li>1 tablespoon beeswax pellets</li>
<li>2 tablespoons coconut oil (or another oil like sweet almond or olive)</li>
<li>1 teaspoon honey</li>
<li>A small heatproof jug or bowl, a saucepan, a spoon</li>
<li>Small clean tins or jars with lids</li></ul>
<p><strong>Safety first:</strong> melted wax and oil get very hot and can burn badly. Always do this with an adult. Don't use this balm if you are allergic to bee products, nuts or any ingredient, and test a small dab on your wrist first.</p>
<h2>Steps</h2><ol>
<li><strong>Set up a water bath.</strong> Put a few centimetres of water in a saucepan and heat it gently. Place the heatproof jug or bowl in the water.</li>
<li><strong>Melt.</strong> Add the beeswax and oil to the jug and stir until the wax has completely melted.</li>
<li><strong>Add the honey.</strong> Take the jug off the heat (an adult should do this with an oven mitt) and stir in the honey.</li>
<li><strong>Keep stirring.</strong> Honey doesn't mix easily with wax and oil, so keep stirring for a minute or two as it starts to cool.</li>
<li><strong>Pour</strong> carefully into your tins and leave them to set for about an hour.</li></ol>
<blockquote>Chemistry you can put in your pocket.</blockquote>
<h2>The science bit</h2><p>Beeswax is solid at room temperature and melts when heated. Mixing it with a liquid oil makes a balm that is soft enough to spread but firm enough to hold its shape. More wax makes it firmer; more oil makes it softer. That's your experiment: try changing the amounts and see what happens.</p>
<h2>Make it a gift</h2><p>Decorate the lids with stickers or a label you design yourself. Keep your balm somewhere cool, and make a fresh batch if it ever smells strange.</p>'''),

dict(slug='make-a-zine', cat='make', img='scissors', support=False,
 title="""Design a zine about your favourite thing""",
 excerpt="""Fold, cut, write and photocopy.""",
 desc="""How to make an eight-page mini zine from one sheet of paper, with ideas for what to put inside.""",
 lead="""A zine (say "zeen") is a small, homemade magazine about anything you love. All you need is one sheet of paper, scissors and something to say.""",
 body='''<h2>What can a zine be about?</h2><p>Literally anything: your favourite band, a book series, frogs, your top ten snacks, tips for looking after a pet, a comic you invented, your city's best parks. Zines can be funny, serious, arty or full of facts. There are no rules, and that's the fun.</p>
<h2>Fold an eight-page mini zine</h2><ol>
<li>Take one sheet of A4 paper and fold it in half the long way. Unfold.</li>
<li>Fold it in half the short way, then in half again, and once more. When you unfold it, you should have eight rectangles.</li>
<li>Fold it in half the short way again. Starting from the folded edge, cut along the middle crease, but only as far as the centre of the page.</li>
<li>Open the paper and fold it in half the long way. Push the two ends towards each other so the cut opens up into a diamond, then a plus shape.</li>
<li>Flatten it into a little booklet. You have a front cover, six inside pages and a back cover.</li></ol>
<p>Tip: practise with scrap paper first, then unfold your finished blank zine to see which way up each page goes before you draw.</p>
<blockquote>Small pages, big ideas.</blockquote>
<h2>Fill the pages</h2><ul><li>Cover: a big title and a drawing.</li><li>Inside: lists, comics, collages, facts, interviews with your family or friends (ask them first!), or a quiz.</li><li>Back: a little doodle, or "The End" in your best lettering.</li></ul>
<p>Mix handwriting, magazine cut-outs and drawings. Black pen copies best if you want to make more.</p>
<h2>Share it safely</h2><p>Want copies for friends? Unfold your finished zine and photocopy it at a library or at home, then fold the copies. Keep your full name, school and address out of anything you hand around. A made-up zine name or a nickname is much cooler anyway.</p>'''),
]
